---
// Front 26 — jhonstart-router · examples/src/app/blog/page.bpp
//
// The streamed blog index of `streamed-blog-page-example.bp`, as a `.bpp` page:
// a header that costs nothing and a post list that costs 900 ms. The shell —
// header, heading, and the skeleton where the list will go — flushes
// immediately; the list arrives as a second chunk.
//
// The boundary is built in the fence and placed with a hole: `Suspense(posts)`
// is an expression that answers an `Element`, and an `Element` in a hole is
// spliced as it is. The boundary's child is a THUNK (`{ -> PostList() }`), not a
// started task — `@Task` is eager on the BEAM, so a boundary holding a started
// task would hold a result computed before the shell was written, and nothing
// would stream. Building the record runs nothing.
//
// `skeleton` is a `fn` of the fence, so it is a function of the module; it
// returns markup with `html`, which the emitted module imports. `PostList` is a
// `.bpp` component of its own (`components/post_list.bpp`): the slow half knows
// nothing about the boundary it sits in.
//
// Upstream:
//   https://nextjs.org/docs/app/guides/streaming
//   https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/file-conventions/loading

import {div, h1, header, section} from "jhonstart";
import {Boundary, Suspense, boundaryId} from "jhonstart";
import {components.post_list as PostList};   // a `.bpp`'s default, named by the importer (288)

// The fallback: what the reader sees while the list is in flight.
fn skeleton() -> Element {
    return html """<div class="skeleton">Loading posts…</div>""";
}

val posts = Boundary(
    id: boundaryId(["blog"], "posts"),
    fallback: skeleton(),
    child: { ->
        PostList();
    },
);
---
<div class="page">
  <header class="site-header">botopink</header>
  <h1>Blog</h1>
  <section class="main">
    {Suspense(posts)}
  </section>
</div>
